"8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74206/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "e5f6e785-bffd-53b0-ba79-56e03699ca7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74206/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "3ccf102a-7163-5255-950a-9b473eb83575" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74206/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3050d997-e3a0-5f7e-ae55-d00fe2325e09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74206/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer-field/choices ["q-74206/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-74206/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-74206/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-74206/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-74206/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74206" :question/id #uuid "aa2317dd-ccab-5589-b882-514074d63c86" :question/math-academy-id "q-74206" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,-3)$ and the line $y=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/2a3107d0db934d4dc9552444badc52914f92d46d7fd83a3e6048d06f3465dcf9.png)\n\nSince the line $y=5$ is a horizontal line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(2,-3)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $y$-coordinate of the point and $5.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-3-5|=8.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-74206/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74206"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-74060"] :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-74439/selection/7090357a881be4d33620bf847a636d29bd7e0bf68deea0064d5d0efe5519ab88" :answer/id #uuid "2a62eee6-f082-50fe-bdb9-3403ccbb2121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74439/selection/2ab0846437ac577c7ad5a341365117bba109724295c61b8542adab253399be53" :answer/id #uuid "9cdbc744-f046-5a85-b525-147f31e4c7e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74439/selection/64b40412b8b548e9db61b40e65e429295db220ca3aa45212f2011c9fabbd9c4b" :answer/id #uuid "125921a3-35e7-53c8-90b8-d8a79d2b91eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74439/selection/a0df205a085a017b83e0565c9c57a6cf159145bbbd452ad77949f13f41582921" :answer/id #uuid "8cc0f3c5-1cb0-5ef8-a0d4-769590b54a27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74439/selection/e03b06bf248cd662e61b3dea2ac75da56aa7449dc8bb6e4b62f259b0801107e1" :answer/id #uuid "74174e90-283c-5547-b985-e78264be4d0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74439/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c1d8ca-5100-5cb9-a5d7-82e3ef59e72e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74439/selection/a0df205a085a017b83e0565c9c57a6cf159145bbbd452ad77949f13f41582921" :answer-field/choices ["q-74439/selection/7090357a881be4d33620bf847a636d29bd7e0bf68deea0064d5d0efe5519ab88" "q-74439/selection/2ab0846437ac577c7ad5a341365117bba109724295c61b8542adab253399be53" "q-74439/selection/64b40412b8b548e9db61b40e65e429295db220ca3aa45212f2011c9fabbd9c4b" "q-74439/selection/a0df205a085a017b83e0565c9c57a6cf159145bbbd452ad77949f13f41582921" "q-74439/selection/e03b06bf248cd662e61b3dea2ac75da56aa7449dc8bb6e4b62f259b0801107e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74439" :question/id #uuid "942dc2e8-6f1d-5b4e-8dc9-4a3941397629" :question/math-academy-id "q-74439" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74439/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74439"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-74061"] :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-114345/selection/ccc923cf3ae94f06228d5826dc5458b25d788223e4a253dc5adc0cb7acbc901d" :answer/id #uuid "d518c73f-060d-5a2e-a88b-ccd0767ccf2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},-\\frac{11}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114345/selection/48be7f022b621f60176b87bf906f289440d1d7bb85c1c5197a6e11c295ffe91d" :answer/id #uuid "f91581f5-1f72-5dae-88c1-f6bcfb29be2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3,11)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114345/selection/99a3e67aa3cd484a62d7478e8836bb78f266570316a160db31dcfec4426e8995" :answer/id #uuid "58f5b96f-8e0f-5d43-be42-2be5f5478d64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114345/selection/8c37f490bd54960328610095caa2e2585a3a60b475d460a573fe50cc541161e4" :answer/id #uuid "400691fb-900d-5318-9bfd-096e5742224a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{13},\\frac{3}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114345/selection/5a298e7bb616ad9b856eaad5e5b5adc5bd6d1dd5ac1d124b26d4344825dc42b5" :answer/id #uuid "416bff52-2ca3-5809-9dd3-06101b21863e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114345/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c6958058-789f-5cc4-aa89-27ad2f2cdceb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114345/selection/99a3e67aa3cd484a62d7478e8836bb78f266570316a160db31dcfec4426e8995" :answer-field/choices ["q-114345/selection/ccc923cf3ae94f06228d5826dc5458b25d788223e4a253dc5adc0cb7acbc901d" "q-114345/selection/48be7f022b621f60176b87bf906f289440d1d7bb85c1c5197a6e11c295ffe91d" "q-114345/selection/99a3e67aa3cd484a62d7478e8836bb78f266570316a160db31dcfec4426e8995" "q-114345/selection/8c37f490bd54960328610095caa2e2585a3a60b475d460a573fe50cc541161e4" "q-114345/selection/5a298e7bb616ad9b856eaad5e5b5adc5bd6d1dd5ac1d124b26d4344825dc42b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114345" :question/id #uuid "3f083c6a-63f4-551e-9ac4-c8f0d3effd8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114345" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114345/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114345"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114336/selection/16cd27ee483c934f4c73da934f9935c7e9a9b6ad2771335f6b85ab9fbbd438c9" :answer/id #uuid "f0ade176-cbf4-587c-a5d8-17992475a05c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114336/selection/915fe780e97bd2355146418a8c7b228c7c8ecbd231892ce66386dbb33fede00c" :answer/id #uuid "479c1d82-775d-5218-a0f2-3f2d9dfb1035" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114336/selection/a7489becb26b4ad57757df3389f76e31e1426eb3e2888a7330c8b8807691c3b0" :answer/id #uuid "e8902a36-ed80-5987-9b7a-267319183572" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{66}{13},-\\frac{18}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114336/selection/0335ddc16b6d215b7a4a2cc78ee9d92c0f58ea1d8b223440f2d072a90f73eb25" :answer/id #uuid "51f5f015-9087-5c4e-b535-bd49c4b2f589" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{90}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114336/selection/33a764af78eb0cc515bd21344f09eda6233318fbbf1f2669ae715a1c1b5c090f" :answer/id #uuid "c78e19f3-70d9-56c1-a6ea-50b787d3887a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},-\\frac{66}{13})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114336/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe32756-a5c1-5bb5-a38a-24fb672f9c74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114336/selection/915fe780e97bd2355146418a8c7b228c7c8ecbd231892ce66386dbb33fede00c" :answer-field/choices ["q-114336/selection/16cd27ee483c934f4c73da934f9935c7e9a9b6ad2771335f6b85ab9fbbd438c9" "q-114336/selection/915fe780e97bd2355146418a8c7b228c7c8ecbd231892ce66386dbb33fede00c" "q-114336/selection/a7489becb26b4ad57757df3389f76e31e1426eb3e2888a7330c8b8807691c3b0" "q-114336/selection/0335ddc16b6d215b7a4a2cc78ee9d92c0f58ea1d8b223440f2d072a90f73eb25" "q-114336/selection/33a764af78eb0cc515bd21344f09eda6233318fbbf1f2669ae715a1c1b5c090f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114336" :question/id #uuid "49e0ab8c-4ebb-5b2d-8e5e-32400720dc1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-114336" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/69e06e1b9b72e3a4c1fc2e7f2fcd85aa4aa8b34e4fa2f4f5aebb8d1b10474415.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(-3,4).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =6-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4)$ is $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114336/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114336"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114341/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b3301e55-6155-51ca-a280-86552f2bdaa3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114341/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer/id #uuid "9ff7d472-b785-5d11-9101-4d8e4f862c6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114341/selection/219833e72de92bafe4f4f5813822a337104a6b771fd9bf741ddcca8750ecab9e" :answer/id #uuid "2b83b79a-3d27-563d-817a-17fce0796043" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114341/selection/7336f16cf9437bd809ce5e09d7dc666eb175d0cf5c31ff077c77f0542ed57f13" :answer/id #uuid "a3ee4382-b711-529c-bafa-5337cfc35ccb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114341/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" :answer/id #uuid "431e99be-c9e2-5789-b4b7-9db2caaab681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114341/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "47c2e812-cb6a-5580-bf28-e45b01817124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114341/selection/7336f16cf9437bd809ce5e09d7dc666eb175d0cf5c31ff077c77f0542ed57f13" :answer-field/choices ["q-114341/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-114341/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" "q-114341/selection/219833e72de92bafe4f4f5813822a337104a6b771fd9bf741ddcca8750ecab9e" "q-114341/selection/7336f16cf9437bd809ce5e09d7dc666eb175d0cf5c31ff077c77f0542ed57f13" "q-114341/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114341" :question/id #uuid "e79577df-8880-5651-be09-d6a38cdd29b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114341" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/3a4bbfd80480fe3fcc778a490514d27d65d83abf77ca1752e4203d9b3185b618.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(1,-5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{17}{3}-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(4,-3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,-5)$ and $(4,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-1)^{2}+(-3+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114341/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114341"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-74417/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "fc889719-669e-5057-a251-e51ea4da88f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74417/selection/c1519fe0443f7c335168d712e07d2fe76542f6c5a578d93f531afd0ceeb21246" :answer/id #uuid "7fc122d5-1d1e-5697-a372-a904ded26a48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74417/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" :answer/id #uuid "18bd2741-5630-52c0-b65b-c084ea181bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74417/selection/bb5e3e524771fed0f8298cd585cdb5759ed07bb1ac0ac034d00b73a0b668a9ce" :answer/id #uuid "f21c6d77-c735-5067-b312-7a5d2c649ff8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74417/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "a6255463-5820-582f-b710-6cb9ce7f0d9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74417/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a95cdb93-c8e3-5a95-a41d-02d781042020" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-74417/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" :answer-field/choices ["q-74417/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-74417/selection/c1519fe0443f7c335168d712e07d2fe76542f6c5a578d93f531afd0ceeb21246" "q-74417/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" "q-74417/selection/bb5e3e524771fed0f8298cd585cdb5759ed07bb1ac0ac034d00b73a0b668a9ce" "q-74417/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-74417" :question/id #uuid "1a823c10-5ebd-503e-9bab-03ff8b0369b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-74417" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/eb1176b4a5e2565fceb03e9af652a6ae875f5ee678c103405045fcebc36342e1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x+2$ and passes through the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+3 & =1(x+1) \\\\ y+3 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+2 \\\\ y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2 & =x-2 \\\\ -x-x & =-2-2 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,-3)$ and $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2$ is $3\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-74417/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-74417"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114358/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "382fb6a8-0da8-565d-8eb4-d71e3bfc3d08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114358/selection/8727795d39f2d853e4d2d4fbc1dee09787f9d50fa3a1a9d433eee05c1fcdbcf5" :answer/id #uuid "9b3a4a11-d48d-5cfe-aa22-5c2868265ad4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{19}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114358/selection/54e998e2b9613564e4db88fa790dea7921b62f7340b79dc7ba61859b983ac0f6" :answer/id #uuid "22435a36-a01f-5169-853b-df19b9d7fd4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114358/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "417e390a-d43f-5f4f-9275-75c5dbd08654" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114358/selection/6cf1ba8fcecf7ff74fcb48e7f39a2cabed6551908e76b1f229ada74cff9b7d1c" :answer/id #uuid "9cd5714e-c909-5212-813a-93836ab3a06f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114358/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "86a13e16-d3cc-55f3-9310-c97d9b334a7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114358/selection/54e998e2b9613564e4db88fa790dea7921b62f7340b79dc7ba61859b983ac0f6" :answer-field/choices ["q-114358/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-114358/selection/8727795d39f2d853e4d2d4fbc1dee09787f9d50fa3a1a9d433eee05c1fcdbcf5" "q-114358/selection/54e998e2b9613564e4db88fa790dea7921b62f7340b79dc7ba61859b983ac0f6" "q-114358/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-114358/selection/6cf1ba8fcecf7ff74fcb48e7f39a2cabed6551908e76b1f229ada74cff9b7d1c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114358" :question/id #uuid "b9d5b1c1-cc35-5675-83c0-9258962522cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-114358" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+5x & =10 \\\\ 2y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/8c4238a00b775d4db828651ae2691596bfadafcdc748494cc957bad1cb1b6b91.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5$ and passes through the point $(-5,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+5) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{2}{5}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{29}{10}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=5.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(0,5).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-5,3)$ and $(0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0-(-5))}^{2}+(5-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10$ is $\\sqrt{29}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-114358/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114358"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114343/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "1a58071a-a50b-5aa6-a200-2e2ac43481a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114343/selection/f1fc99d76de679b25d7bc8a032d05611ddde6c3060e9504759d4c7dadb0e382d" :answer/id #uuid "ea60e65c-7c57-52c4-8046-97d28472df67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114343/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" :answer/id #uuid "29cba391-82ec-586c-adbb-471250c9da41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114343/selection/219833e72de92bafe4f4f5813822a337104a6b771fd9bf741ddcca8750ecab9e" :answer/id #uuid "36bebfca-66e3-536e-b2d7-230ae61652b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114343/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "16fd2dc9-2117-5499-971e-3acc531cba0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114343/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e728ae88-bf1b-58c8-968b-04a4a468a486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114343/selection/219833e72de92bafe4f4f5813822a337104a6b771fd9bf741ddcca8750ecab9e" :answer-field/choices ["q-114343/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-114343/selection/f1fc99d76de679b25d7bc8a032d05611ddde6c3060e9504759d4c7dadb0e382d" "q-114343/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" "q-114343/selection/219833e72de92bafe4f4f5813822a337104a6b771fd9bf741ddcca8750ecab9e" "q-114343/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114343" :question/id #uuid "c8a6f517-e603-56a5-8d4e-8cbb84ce1587" :question/math-academy-id "q-114343" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3+1)^{2}+(1-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2$ is $4\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114343/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114343"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114364/selection/7336f16cf9437bd809ce5e09d7dc666eb175d0cf5c31ff077c77f0542ed57f13" :answer/id #uuid "79a76960-b7f7-581e-a8ac-8e282e88b1ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114364/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "79b8872a-1686-5334-bff8-879d30f80ce8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114364/selection/4ef0fcc340e40ad6bcef1c832215cd28abf240aabeed43296dfece28d20d1935" :answer/id #uuid "1d9f84bd-11d4-5ee3-8592-2e261036f7d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114364/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" :answer/id #uuid "a0ba03f5-5011-5c62-abe1-b762cb17cff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114364/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "11aa50df-2313-5319-bb0f-e046cca3d043" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114364/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e3ceee1-bacb-5d42-91fb-82c9f4a68f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114364/selection/7336f16cf9437bd809ce5e09d7dc666eb175d0cf5c31ff077c77f0542ed57f13" :answer-field/choices ["q-114364/selection/7336f16cf9437bd809ce5e09d7dc666eb175d0cf5c31ff077c77f0542ed57f13" "q-114364/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-114364/selection/4ef0fcc340e40ad6bcef1c832215cd28abf240aabeed43296dfece28d20d1935" "q-114364/selection/05402db46107d70ece210f0bd5e24b332ae4f43bd474d592035c3e3c92625629" "q-114364/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114364" :question/id #uuid "93104da6-8eb0-5ad5-8b24-6e007223800c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114364" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+3x & =-6 \\\\ 2y & =-3x-6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/715e1292edaf78979f6b9ae36586a8196dcc30e865915e7eefba2360281f85ec.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3$ and passes through the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{5}{6}+3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-2)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-114364/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114364"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
